rt of the expansion from the first equation. So the second equation is equal to that sum, which is equal to 25 - 3mnp. Wait, let me check:\n\nFrom the first equation, (m + n + p)(mn + mp + np) equals that sum plus 3mnp. Therefore, the sum m²n + m²p + mn² + n²p + mp² + np² is equal to 25 - 3mnp.\n\nBut the second equation is equal to that same sum. So:\n\nm²(n + p) + n²(m + p) + p²(m + n) = sum = 25 - 3mnp.\n\nBut according to the problem, this sum is equal to 4. Therefore:\n\n25 - 3mnp = 4.\n\nSo solving for mnp:\n\n25 - 4 = 3mnp => 21 = 3mnp => mnp = 7.\n\nWait, so is that it? That seems straightforward. Let me check again.\n\nLet me verify the steps.\n\nFirst, expand (m + n + p)(mn + mp + np). The expansion includes all the terms m²n, m²p, etc., plus 3mnp. Then, the second equation is exactly the sum of m²n + m²p + mn² + n²p + mp² + np². Therefore, that sum is equal to (m + n + p)(mn + mp + np) - 3mnp. Which equals 25 - 3mnp.\n\nTherefore, setting that equal to 4:\n\n25 - 3mnp = 4 => 3mnp = 21 => mnp = 7.\n\nSo the answer should be 7? That seems too straightforward. Let me check if I made any mistake in the expansion.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check with some numbers. Suppose m, n, p are numbers such that mnp = 7. Let me try to find such numbers. But maybe that would take time. Alternatively, maybe I can think of another way.\n\nAlternatively, let me denote S = m + n + p, P = mn + mp + np, Q = mnp. Then the first equation is S*P = 25.\n\nThe second equation is m²(n + p) + n²(m + p) + p²(m + n). Let me see:\n\nNote that n + p = S - m, similarly for others. Therefore, the second equation becomes:\n\nm²(S - m) + n²(S - n) + p²(S - p) = 4.\n\nExpanding this:\n\nSm² - m³ + Sn² - n³ + Sp² - p³ = 4.\n\nSo S(m² + n² + p²) - (m³ + n³ + p³) = 4.\n\nBut I also know that m² + n² + p² can be written in terms of S and P. Since (m + n + p)^2 = m² + n² + p² + 2(mn + mp + np), so m² + n² + p² = S² - 2P.\n\nAlso, m³ + n³ + p³ can be expressed using the identity:\n\nm³ + n³ + p³