alf of that, which is 12 cm. So that\'s 12 cm.\n\nSo the apex is 15 cm from each vertex, which is 15 cm from a point that is 12 cm away from the center. Wait, so if I imagine a right triangle where one leg is the height of the pyramid (h), and the other leg is 12 cm (distance from center to vertex), and the hypotenuse is 15 cm (distance from apex to vertex). Then by Pythagoras:\n\nh² + 12² = 15²\n\nSo h² + 144 = 225\n\nTherefore, h² = 225 - 144 = 81\n\nSo h = sqrt(81) = 9 cm.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. So the height is 9 cm. Then volume is (1/3)*288*9.\n\nLet me compute that. First, 288 divided by 3 is 96. Then 96 times 9. Let me do 96*9. Well, 100*9=900, minus 4*9=36, so 900-36=864. So the volume is 864 cubic centimeters.\n\nBut wait, let me check again. Maybe I made a mistake in the distance from the center to the vertex?\n\nWait, the base is a square with area 288. So if the area is s² = 288, then s = sqrt(288) = 12√2, yes. Then the diagonal of the square is s√2, so 12√2 * √2 = 12*2 = 24, correct. So half of that is 12. So the center is 12 cm from each vertex. Then the apex is 15 cm from each vertex. So the height of the pyramid is sqrt(15² - 12²) = sqrt(225 - 144) = sqrt(81) = 9. That seems correct. Then Volume is (1/3)*base area*height = (1/3)*288*9. Let me check that again. 288 divided by 3 is 96. 96*9 is 864. So that\'s 864.\n\nWait, but I have a doubt here. Is the apex really 15 cm from the vertices? Let me make sure I didn\'t mix up the distance. The problem says "its peak is 15 cm from each of the other vertices." So the apex is 15 cm away from each of the four corners of the base. So yes, that\'s the distance from the apex to each vertex. So that is the hypotenuse of the right triangle with legs h and 12 cm. So that gives h = 9 cm. Then the volume is 864. That seems right. Let me check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute the slant height or something else. But no, since the apex is directly above the center, the distance from apex to base vertex is sqrt( (height)^2 + (distance from center to vertex)^2 ). Which is exactly what I did. So that seems correct.\n\nTherefore, the volume is 864. Wait, is there a chance that the distance from the apex to the base vertex is not along the line? No, the problem says the peak is 15 cm from each of the other vertices, so that distance is straight line. So the calculation is correct.\n\nTherefore, Answer: \\boxed{864}\n</think>\n\nTo find the volume of a right pyramid, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Volume} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the side length of the square base\n\nThe base area is given as 288 square centimeters. Since the base is a square, the area $ A $ is related to the side length $ s $ by:\n\n$$\nA = s^2 = 288 \\Rightarrow s = \\sqrt{288} = 12\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine the distance from the center of the base to a vertex\n\nIn a square, the diagonal length $ d $ is related to the side length $ s $ by:\n\n$$\nd = s\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nThe distance from the center of the square to one of its vertices is half of this:\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{2} = \\frac{s\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{12\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{12 \\cdot 2}{2} = 12 \\text{ cm}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Use the given distance from the apex to a vertex to find the height\n\nWe are told that the apex (vertex of the pyramid) is 15 cm from each of the base vertices. This forms a right triangle where:\n\n- One leg is the height $ h $ of the pyramid (what we need to find),\n- The other leg is the 12 cm from the center of the base to a vertex,\n- The hypotenuse is the 15 cm from the apex to a vertex.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\nh^2 + 12^2 = 15^2 \\Rightarrow h^2 + 144 = 225 \\Rightarrow h^2 = 81 \\Rightarrow h = \\sqrt{81} = 9 \\text{ cm}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the volume of the pyramid\n\n$$\n\\text{Volume} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 288 \\times 9 = \\frac{288 \\times 9}{3} = \\frac{2592}{3} = 864\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{864}\n$$<|im_end|>'], label: 864, reward: 1
